<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="87a3969a">&lt;p&gt;
    Preferably, have all stakeholders meet with the team, in person, to discuss and agree upon the required conditions that
    every element of the product must meet. It is important to include and allow all interested parties to participate so
    there is a common understanding shared among the entire group. This shared understanding will also allow for the
    Definition of Ready and the Definition of Done to more accurately reflect the group&apos;s expectations of product quality.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The entire process is typically moderated by a skilled facilitator, such as a&amp;nbsp;Scrum Master. Gather all interested
    parties and run the meeting with the following agenda:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Introduction&lt;/strong&gt; - a round of introductions may be needed if the participants are not familiar with
        each other. Afterward, the facilitator reiterates the purpose of the DoR and DoD to set the direction for the
        meeting as well as reinforce differentiation between:
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li style=&quot;LIST-STYLE-TYPE: none&quot;&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Overall quality criteria - which may be included in the DoR or DoD, and
            &lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Functional criteria - which should be included as acceptance criteria
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Generation of ideas&lt;/strong&gt; - facilitator asks participants to generate ideas for the conditions to be
        included in the DoR and DoD. For example, this could be done via a collective brainstorming session or by writing
        on post-its individually before sharing them with the group. If there are more than 12 participants, this can still
        be done as a group activity, simply place participants into groups of 5-8 people to make the process more
        manageable.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Sharing of ideas&lt;/strong&gt; - after ideas have been gathered, each participant shares their ideas with
        everyone and the group discusses whether to include them or not. During this discussion, the group also agrees on
        precise wording for each condition. This might be done in two iterations if participants were originally split into
        smaller groups (first share and discuss in smaller groups, then report back to each other).
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="afa59c9a"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="6fdae805">&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        If an in-person meeting cannot be held, other means of communication can be used (e.g., email, conference call with
        facilitation tools, etc.); however, in-person discussions are strongly encouraged.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The applicable criteria will depend on the nature of the work being performed.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The group may consider the Backlog Items, as outlined in the Product Backlog, when drafting the list of criteria
        for the DoR and DoD.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="dcfb908b"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="a6a920d2">To clearly outline the set of conditions agreed by all interested parties so as to confirm alignment and provide
transparency about acceptable product quality.</attribute>
<attribute name="alternatives" nodeId="e31e7c7b"></attribute>
</Element>
